hadiths[51]{hadithnumber,text}:
"520","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) made obligatory the Sadaqat al-Fitr after Ramadan upon the people: one sa’ of dates or one sa’ of barley, upon every free and slave, male and female, who is a Muslim."
"521","Abu Sa'id Al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) said: In the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), we used to give Sadaqat al-Fitr on behalf of every young and old, free and slave, one sa’ of wheat or one sa’ of cheese or barley or dates or raisins. Then when Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) came for Hajj or 'Umrah, he addressed the people from the pulpit and said: I think that two mudd (i.e., half a sa’) of the wheat of Syria is equal to one sa’ of dates (i.e., in value). So the people accepted this. But Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I will continue to give what I used to give (i.e., one sa’) as long as I live. (Subhan Allah, this was adherence to the hadith and aversion to opinion and analogy.)"
"522","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered the giving of Sadaqat al-Fitr before going out for the (Eid) prayer."
"523","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: I do not wish that the mountain of Uhud be turned into gold for me and that even a single dinar remains with me for more than three days, except that I keep that dinar to pay off a debt to someone."
"524","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: O assembly of women! Give charity and seek forgiveness, for I have seen that women are the majority of the inhabitants of Hell. An intelligent woman asked: O Messenger of Allah! What is the reason that women are more in Hell? He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: They curse a lot and are ungrateful to their husbands. I have not seen anyone more deficient in intelligence and religion, and more capable of making a wise man lose his senses, than you. That woman asked: What is the deficiency in our intelligence and religion? He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The deficiency in intelligence is that the testimony of two women is equal to the testimony of one man. And the deficiency in religion is that a woman does not pray for several days (every month due to menstruation) and does not fast in Ramadan (during the days of menstruation)."
"525","`Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that` Allah Almighty says: O son of Adam! Spend, and I will spend on you. And (the Noble Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that Allah’s hand is full; spending day and night does not decrease it in the least."
"526","Sayyiduna Haritha bin Wahb رضي الله عنه narrates that I heard the Messenger of Allah صلى الله عليه وسلم saying: ""Give charity. A time is near when a person will go out with his charity, and the one to whom he offers it will say, 'If you had brought it yesterday, I would have accepted it, but today I am not in need of it.' Thus, no one will be found to accept it."""
"527","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Near the Day of Judgment, the earth will throw out its treasures like great pillars of gold and silver. Then the murderer will come and say, ""It was for this that I committed murder."" And the one who broke ties of kinship will come and say, ""It was for this that I severed my family relations."" And the thief will come and say, ""It was for this that my hand was cut off."" Then all of them will leave it and none will take anything from it."
"528","Lady Zainab, the wife of Abdullah bin Mas’ud (may Allah be pleased with them), says: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said, “O assembly of women! Give charity, even if it is from your jewelry.” She said: Then I came to my husband, Abdullah bin Mas’ud (may Allah be pleased with them), and I said, “You are a poor, empty-handed man, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) has said that we should give charity. So go to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and ask him: If I give it to you and the charity is fulfilled, then good; otherwise, I will give it to someone else.” Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) said, “You go yourself and ask the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him).” Then I went, and an Ansari woman was standing at the door of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him); her purpose was the same as mine. And the awe of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was very great. Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) came out, so we said, “Go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and inform him that two women are at the door asking: If they give charity to their husbands and to the orphans whom they support, will it be accepted as charity or not? And do not tell the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) who we are.” Lady Zainab said: Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) went and asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), so he said, “Who are they?” Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) replied, “One is a woman from the Ansar and the other is Zainab.” He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, “Which Zainab?” He said, “The wife of Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him).” Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him), “They will have a double reward in this: one for maintaining ties of kinship and the other for giving charity.”"
"529","`Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) says that` Abu Talhah Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) was very wealthy in Madinah, and his most beloved wealth was a garden called Bairuha, which was in front of the Prophet’s Mosque. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to go into it and drink its sweet water. Sayyiduna Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said that when this verse was revealed: “You will not attain righteousness until you spend from that which you love in the way of Allah,” Sayyiduna Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up and submitted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): Allah Almighty says, “You will not attain righteousness until you spend from your beloved wealth,” and of all my wealth, Bairuha is the most beloved to me. It is charity in the way of Allah, and I hope for its reward from Allah and for it to be stored for me in the Hereafter. So, O Messenger of Allah, dispose of it wherever you wish. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: This is profitable wealth! This is profitable wealth! I have heard what you said, and I think it appropriate that you distribute it among your relatives. Then Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) distributed it among his relatives and paternal cousins."
"530","It is narrated from Sayyida Maymunah bint Harith (may Allah be pleased with her) that she freed a slave-girl during the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and mentioned this to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: ""If you had given her to your maternal uncles, it would have been a greater cause of reward for you."""
"531","Lady Asma bint Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with her) says: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that my mother has come and she is disinterested in the religion (in other narrations it is mentioned that she is a polytheist), so should I treat her kindly and do good to her? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) replied: Yes."
"532","Narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that a man came and asked the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him): ""My mother died suddenly and did not have the chance to make a will. If she had spoken, she would have given charity. If I give charity on her behalf, will she receive the reward?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Yes."""
"533","‘Sayyiduna Jarir bin Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) says that at the beginning of the day, we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). Some people came who were barefoot, naked, wearing leather sheets around their necks, with their swords hanging, most of them, in fact all of them, were from the tribe of Mudar. Seeing their poverty and destitution, the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) changed. He (peace and blessings be upon him) went inside and then came out. (That is, he became distressed. Subhan Allah, what compassion and sympathy he had!) And he ordered Sayyiduna Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) to call the adhan, then the takbir was said, and the prayer was performed, and the sermon was delivered, and this verse was recited: “O people! Fear Allah, Who created you from a single soul...” (He recited this so that it would be known that all the children of Adam are brothers to each other) ... until the end of the verse. Then he recited this verse from Surah Al-Hashr: “O you who believe! Fear Allah, and let every soul look to what it has sent forth for tomorrow...” (Then the market of charity became active.) Someone gave a gold coin, someone gave a dirham, someone started giving a sa’ of wheat, and someone a sa’ of dates, until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Even if it is only a piece of a date (bring it as charity).” Then a man from the Ansar brought a bag, so much so that his hand became tired, in fact, it became exhausted. Then the people began to give in succession, until I saw two heaps of food and clothing, so much (charity was collected) that I saw the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) shining, as if it were gold, like pure gold. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “Whoever initiates a good practice in Islam (that is, something from the Book and Sunnah), for him is the reward of his own action and the reward of those who act upon it after him, without their reward being diminished in the least. And whoever introduces a bad practice in Islam (that is, something which the Book and Sunnah have prohibited), upon him is the burden of his own action and the burden of those who act upon it after him, without their burden being diminished in the least.”"
"534","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man heard a voice in a cloud in the open field saying, “Water the garden of so-and-so.” (After this voice) the cloud moved to one side and rained water on a rocky land. One of the channels among its channels became completely full. So that man followed the water as it flowed, and suddenly he saw a man standing in his garden, diverting the water here and there with his spade. He said to the owner of the garden, “O servant of Allah! What is your name?” He replied, “So-and-so,” the same name that was heard in the cloud. Then the garden owner said to this man, “O servant of Allah! Why did you ask my name?” He replied, “I heard a voice in the cloud, someone was saying, ‘Water the garden of so-and-so,’ and he mentioned your name. So, what gratitude do you show for Allah’s favor in this garden?” The garden owner said, “Since you have said this, now I will tell you. I see whatever income comes from this garden, I give one third of it in charity, one third my wife and children eat, and one third I spend on the garden.” (From this hadith, it is understood that spending one third of wealth in the way of Allah is better, and it is also known that angels, by the command of Allah Almighty, cause rain to fall—at one place more, at another less.) In another narration, it is stated: “I spend one third on the poor, the beggars, and the travelers.”"
"535","Sayyiduna ‘Adi bin Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) mentioned Hell and turned his face away out of dislike. Then he said, “Protect yourselves from the Fire (the Fire of Hell).” Then he turned his face away out of dislike, until we thought that he (peace and blessings be upon him) was looking at it (the Fire of Hell). Then he (peace and blessings be upon him) said, “Protect yourselves from Hell—even if it be with half a date (by giving it in charity). If someone does not find even that, then (let him protect himself) by saying a good word.”"
"536","`Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that` indeed, whoever gives to a household such a she-camel that provides a large bowl full of milk in the morning and evening, then he will have a great reward."
"537","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he (peace be upon him) said: There are seven types of people whom Allah Almighty will grant shade (i.e., under the Throne) on the Day when there will be no shade except His shade. First, a just ruler (who judges according to the Book and Sunnah, whether he is a king or a governor); second, a youth who grows up in the worship of Allah; third, a person whose heart remains attached to the mosque; fourth, two people who love each other for the sake of Allah, meet for His sake and part for His sake; fifth, a man (such a pious man) whom a woman of noble lineage and wealth invites to commit adultery, and he says, ""I fear Allah"" (and refrains from adultery); sixth, a person who gives charity so secretly that his right hand does not know what his left hand has spent (there is some variation in this wording; the correct version is that the left hand does not know what the right hand has spent); seventh, a person who remembers Allah in solitude and his eyes overflow with tears (due to love or fear of Allah)."
"538","Sidna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) said that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and asked, ""O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! Which charity is the best and most rewarding?"" He (peace and blessings be upon him) replied, ""That you give charity while you are healthy and eager, fearing poverty and hoping for wealth. And do not delay giving charity until the soul reaches the throat and you begin to say, 'This is for so-and-so, and this wealth is for so-and-so,' while it has already become the property of so-and-so (i.e., as soon as you die, your heirs will take it)."""
"539","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah, may Allah be pleased with him, who states: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: Whoever gives charity from his pure (lawful) earnings, even if it is a single date, Allah Almighty takes it in His right hand and nurtures it as one of you nurtures his colt or young she-camel, until it (the charity of a single date) becomes like a mountain or even greater than that."
"540","Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: O people! Allah is pure (i.e., free from all defects and deficiencies) and accepts only that which is pure (i.e., lawful). And Allah has commanded the believers with the same command that He gave to the Messengers. He said: ""O Messengers! Eat of the good things and do righteous deeds. Indeed, I am Knowing of what you do."" And He said: ""O you who believe! Eat of the good things which We have provided for you."" Then he mentioned a man who undertakes a long journey, disheveled and dusty, and he raises his hands towards the sky and says: O Lord, O Lord! While his food is unlawful, his drink is unlawful, his clothing is unlawful, and he is nourished with unlawful things—so how can his supplication be accepted?"
"541","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Muslim women! None of you should consider insignificant the gift to her neighbor, even if it is only a sheep’s hoof. (That is, neither should the recipient reject it considering it insignificant, nor should the giver refrain from giving it out of embarrassment.)"
"542","Sayyiduna Abu Mas’ud (may Allah be pleased with him) says: We were commanded to give charity, and we used to carry loads (to earn for it). Sayyiduna Abu Aqil (may Allah be pleased with him) gave half a sa’ in charity (i.e., about one and a quarter seer), and another person gave more than that. So the hypocrites began to say: Allah has no need of this one’s charity, and the other has only given charity to show off. Then this verse was revealed: “Those who criticize the believers who give charity willingly, and those who find nothing to give except their effort (wages)…”"
"543","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever spends a pair (i.e., two coins, or two rupees, or two dinars), he will be called in Paradise: O servant of Allah! Come here, for here is goodness and virtue for you. Then, the one who is habitual in prayer will be called from the gate of prayer, and the one who is habitual in jihad will be called from the gate of jihad, and the one who is habitual in charity will be called from the gate of charity, and the one who is habitual in fasting will be called from the gate of fasting. So Sayyiduna Abu Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him) submitted: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! There is no need for anyone to be called from all the gates, but will there be anyone who will be called from all the gates? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, and I hope (by the grace of Allah) that you will be among them."
"544","`Sayyiduna Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that` he (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Every good deed is charity."
"545","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) that some companions came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)! The wealthy have taken all the reward, for they pray as we pray, fast as we fast, and give charity from their surplus wealth."" The Prophet (peace be upon him) said, ""Has not Allah made for you means of charity? Every tasbih (saying Subhan Allah) is charity, every takbir is charity, every tahmid (saying Alhamdulillah) is charity, every saying of (La ilaha illallah) is charity, enjoining good is charity, forbidding evil is charity, and fulfilling the right of one's spouse is charity."" The people said, ""O Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him)! If one of us fulfills his desire (i.e., has relations with his wife), is there reward for him in that?"" He (peace be upon him) said, ""Do you not see, if he were to fulfill it in a forbidden way, would he not bear a sin? Likewise, when he fulfills it in a lawful way, he will have a reward."""
"546","Narrated from Umm al-Mu'mineen Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every human body has three hundred and sixty joints. So whoever says ""Allahu Akbar,"" ""Alhamdulillah,"" ""La ilaha illallah,"" ""Subhanallah,"" ""Astaghfirullah,"" removes a stone from people's path, or removes a thorn or a bone from the path, or teaches a good word or commands it, or forbids an evil thing, then this is equal to the count of the three hundred and sixty joints (that is, it is equal to giving thanks for them), and on that day he walks in such a state that he has freed himself from Hell. Abu Tawbah added in his narration that he reaches the evening in this state (that he is freed from Hell)."
"547","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: A man said, ""Tonight I will give some charity."" He took his charity and went out (he intended to conceal the charity, so he went out at night) and gave it into the hand of an adulterous woman. In the morning, people began to talk that last night someone gave charity to an adulterous woman. He said, ""O Allah! All praise is for You, my charity ended up with an adulteress."" Then he said, ""Today I will give charity again."" He went out and gave it to a wealthy person. In the morning, people began to talk that today someone gave charity to a rich man. He said, ""O Allah! All praise is for You, my charity ended up with a wealthy person."" On the third day, he said again, ""I will give charity."" He went out and gave charity into the hand of a thief. In the morning, people began to talk that today someone gave charity to a thief. He said, ""O Allah! All praise is for You, my charity ended up with an adulterous woman, a wealthy person, and a thief."" Then a person came to him (meaning an angel or the prophet of that time) and said to him, ""All your charities have been accepted. The adulteress, so that perhaps she may refrain from adultery (because she committed adultery for her livelihood); as for the wealthy person, it was accepted so that perhaps he may feel ashamed and take a lesson that others give charity, so he too should give, and spend from the wealth Allah has given him; and the thief, so that perhaps he may refrain from stealing (because today his need was fulfilled)."""
"548","Sayyiduna Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The example of a miser and a charitable person is like two men wearing iron armor. When the generous person wants to give charity, his armor becomes loose, until it begins to erase the marks of his footsteps. And when the miser wants to give charity, his armor becomes tight, and his hands get stuck to his neck, and each ring gets locked into the next ring. The narrator of the hadith (Sayyiduna Abu Huraira, may Allah be pleased with him) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say that then he tries to loosen it, but it does not become loose."
"549","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every day two angels descend. One says: O Allah! Give more to the one who spends, and the other says: O Allah! Destroy the wealth of the miser."
"550","Abu Musa Ash'ari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates from the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: The Muslim trustworthy treasurer, who spends, and sometimes he (the Prophet) said: who gives what he has been commanded to give, and gives the full amount (i.e., does not deduct anything as commission or bribe), and gives the full charity, with happiness of heart, and delivers it to the one he has been ordered to give it to—he too is considered as one who gives charity."
"551","Narrated from Sayyida Asma bint Abi Bakr Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with her) that she came to the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), I have nothing except what Zubair (may Allah be pleased with him) gives me. If I give some of it in charity, will I be sinful?"" He (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Give as much as you can, do not hoard it, and do not keep reminding (of your charity), otherwise Allah will also withhold from you."""
"552","‘Umm al-Mu’minin Aisha Siddiqa (may Allah be pleased with her) says that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When a woman spends from the grain of her house without causing corruption (that is, as is customary, such as giving a piece to a poor person or a handful to a beggar, in which the husband’s consent is known by custom), then she will have the reward for what she spent, and the husband will have the reward for what he earned. And the treasurer will also have the same reward, so that the reward of one will not diminish the reward of the other (that is, Allah Almighty will give each one a separate reward, not that He will make one share in the reward of the other)."
"553","‘Sayyiduna Umair, who was the freed slave of Sayyiduna Abi Al-Lahm (may Allah be pleased with him), says: My master (Abi Al-Lahm) ordered me to dry some meat. A poor man came, so I gave him an amount suitable for eating. When my master found out, he beat me. I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and informed him (Glory be to Allah, you were a refuge for orphans, widows, and the oppressed). The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) called him and asked: Why did you beat him? He replied: He gives away my food without my permission. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Both of you will have the reward for this charity."
"554","´Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that` the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: No woman should observe (optional) fast while her husband is present at home except with his permission, and she should not allow anyone (of her mahram relatives) to enter his house while he is present except with his permission (and when he is not present, then all the more she should not allow anyone to enter except according to his prior instructions and consent). And whatever she spends from his earnings without his permission, then in that (reward) her husband has half the reward (i.e., the man gets the reward for earning and the woman for giving)."
"555","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) that some people from the Ansar asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) for something, so he gave them. They asked again, and he gave them again, until whatever he had was finished. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whatever wealth I have, I do not withhold it from you. And whoever refrains from asking, Allah will protect him; and whoever makes himself self-sufficient, Allah will make him self-sufficient; and whoever strives to be patient, Allah will grant him patience. And no gift from Allah is better and more expansive than patience."""
"556","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Amr bin al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed, successful is the person who accepted Islam, was given sustenance according to his need, and Allah made him content with whatever He gave him."
"557","Sayyiduna Muawiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Avoid clinging to asking (begging), for by Allah, if someone asks me for something and, because of his request, something is spent from me and I dislike it (i.e., I give it unwillingly), then how can there be any blessing in it?"
"558","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One of you will continue asking (begging) until he meets Allah and there will not be a single piece of flesh on his face."
"559","Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) says that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: If someone goes in the morning and carries a bundle of wood on his back, and gives charity from it and fulfills his own needs so that he is not dependent on people, this is better for him than going around asking people, whether they give him or not. And indeed, the upper (giving) hand is better than the lower (receiving) hand, and first give charity to the one whose expenses are your responsibility."
"560","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said—and at that time he (peace and blessings be upon him) was on the pulpit mentioning charity and refraining from asking from others. He (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The upper hand is better than the lower hand, and the upper hand is the one that gives, while the lower hand is the one that asks."
"561","Sayyiduna Hakim bin Hizam (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for wealth, and he gave it to me. I asked again, and he gave it to me again. I asked again, and he gave it to me again. Then he said: This wealth is lush and sweet. So whoever takes it without asking for it, or takes it with the giver’s pleasure (not by forcefully demanding it), then there is blessing in it. And whoever takes it by humiliating himself (i.e., by begging or clinging), then there is no blessing in it, and his wealth is such that a person eats from it but is not satisfied. And the upper hand is better than the lower hand."
"562","Narrated from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The needy is not the one who goes around and returns with one or two morsels or one or two dates. The Companions asked, ""O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then who is the needy?"" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who does not get enough to suffice his basic human needs, and people do not consider him needy so that they would give him charity, nor does he ask anything from people."
"563","Abu Huraira (may Allah be pleased with him) reports that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Wealth is not in having many possessions, but true wealth is the richness of the heart."""
"564","`Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates that` the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The son of Adam (human being) grows old, but two things remain young in him: the desire for wealth and the desire for a long life."
"565","Sayyiduna Abu’l-Aswad said that Sayyiduna Abu Musa Ash‘ari (may Allah be pleased with him) summoned the reciters of Basra, and all three hundred reciters who had completed the recitation of the Qur’an came to him. Sayyiduna Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) said to them: “You are the best among the people of Basra and its reciters, so continue to recite the Qur’an and do not become lazy after a long time has passed, lest your hearts become hardened as the hearts of those before you became hardened. And we used to recite a surah which, in length and in stern warnings, was equal to ‘Bara’ah’ (Surah At-Tawbah). Then I was made to forget it, except for this part which I remember: ‘If a man had two valleys full of wealth, he would seek a third, and nothing fills the belly of man except dust.’ And we used to recite another surah, which we considered equal to one of the Musabbihat (the surahs beginning with ‘Glorify’), but I have forgotten it too, except for this verse which I remember: ‘O you who believe! Why do you say what you do not do?’ (If you say such a thing which you do not do yourself,) it will be written against your necks as a testimony, and you will be questioned about it on the Day of Resurrection.”"
"566","Sayyiduna Abu Sa'id Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) said that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) stood and preached to the people and said: O people! By Allah, I do not fear anything for you except that which Allah brings forth as the adornment of this world for you. Then a man said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! Can good ever result in evil? (That is, the possession of worldly wealth and power and the progress of Islam is good, so how can its result be bad?) Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) remained silent for a while. Then he said: What did you say? (He asked about his question again so that if he had forgotten, the answer would not be inconsistent with the question and he would not understand.) He said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! Can good ever result in evil? The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: No, the result of good is only good. Is this good? (That is, the Prophet's intention was that this is a test and trial, and where is the good in a test and trial?) Then he explained by giving an example: In the spring season, the greenery that grows, that greenery destroys the animals except those animals that eat only as much as they need. They eat to the extent that their bellies become full. Then they come into the sun and pass dung or urine, chew the cud, and then go and start grazing again. So whoever takes wealth with its due right, there is blessing placed in it for him. And whoever takes it unlawfully, his example is like that animal which eats but is never satisfied (and thus brings about its own destruction)."
"567","It is narrated from Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to give some wealth to Sayyiduna Umar bin Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), so Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) would say, ""O Messenger of Allah! Please give it to someone who is more in need than me."" So the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said, ""Take this wealth, and whether you keep it with you or give it in charity, it is up to you. And whatever of this kind of wealth comes to you, and you neither desired it nor asked for it, then take it. But for that which is not like this, do not let your soul pursue it."" Salim said that because of this, Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) would not ask anything from anyone, and if someone gave him something, he would not refuse it."
"568","Sayyiduna Qabeesah bin Mukhaariq Hilaali (may Allah be pleased with him) says: I became indebted with a large amount (i.e., for reconciling between two tribes or for some other good cause), so I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and asked him (for help). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Wait until the charity funds come to us, then I will order that something be given to you from it. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: O Qabeesah, asking (for charity) is not lawful except for three persons. One is the person who becomes indebted (in a good cause), so asking becomes lawful for him until he receives enough wealth to meet his needs, then he should refrain from asking. The second is the person whose wealth is struck by calamity and his wealth is destroyed, so asking becomes lawful for him until he receives enough to meet his needs (or he said the word ""sadaadan""). The third is the person who is afflicted by hunger, and three wise men from his people testify that he is indeed afflicted by hunger, so asking is also permissible for him until he finds enough to meet his needs. And apart from these people, O Qabeesah, asking is forbidden; and whoever asks besides these (categories), he is consuming what is unlawful."
"569","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) says that I was walking with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he was wearing a cloak from Najran (a city) which had a thick border. Suddenly, a Bedouin came and pulled the cloak from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so forcefully that I saw the mark left by the cloak on the vertebra of his neck. Then he said, ""O Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)! Order that I be given something from the wealth which Allah has given you."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked towards him and smiled, and ordered that he be given something."
"570","Narrated from Sayyiduna Miswar bin Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) distributed sheets and did not give any to Sayyiduna Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him). Then Sayyiduna Makhramah (may Allah be pleased with him) said, ""O my son! Go with me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."" So I went with him, and he said, ""Go into the house and call the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."" I called the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he came out wearing one of those sheets, and said, ""I had kept this for you."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at Makhramah and said, ""Makhramah became happy."""
